text:
noah: quick note — the new minimal-next-langchain starter is tripping the eval harness intermittently when run with edge runtime. putting repro here.

pri: thanks, what are the symptoms?

noah: often a 401 during token rotation, then the SDK retry path appears to replay with the stale token and the stream is cut off. harness sees truncated generation.

chloe: which SDK version does the starter pin?

noah: package.json pinned @redwood/sdk@0.12.1 in the template. we shipped a refresh-concurrency fix in 0.12.3.

pri: two vectors: edge runtime vs node env surface, and SDK parity. edge envs sometimes lack process.env at runtime or expose different vars, so Authorization header can be empty if folks follow the example verbatim.

chloe: can you paste the api handler code in the starter?

noah: yup — small handler:```js
export const runtime = 'edge';
export default async function handler(req) {
  const { prompt } = await req.json();
  const r = await fetch(process.env.REDWOOD_URL + '/v1/chat', {
    method: 'POST',
    headers: { Authorization: `Bearer ${process.env.REDWOOD_KEY}` },
    body: JSON.stringify({ prompt })
  });
  return new Response(await r.text(), { status: r.status });
}
```

pri: ok. low-friction fixes: bump the starter to @redwood/sdk@0.12.3, add a guard at top of handler that throws a helpful error if REDWOOD_KEY is missing, and add a small README note "Edge runtime gotchas" so new users know.

chloe: also add logging when Authorization is empty to help debug in CI/harness. something like:```js
if (!process.env.REDWOOD_KEY) {
  console.error('REDWOOD_KEY missing — set .env.local or use server runtime');
  throw new Error('REDWOOD_KEY missing: see README for edge gotchas');
}
```

noah: for the harness side, can we change the eval harness to retry 401 with exponential backoff up to 3 attempts? currently any non-200 fails immediately which makes token-rotation races noisy.

pri: yes — we should treat 401 as potentially transient during rotation. small change, big reduction in flakes.

chloe: propose adding a tiny msw unit test in the starter repo: first /v1/chat returns 401, token refresh endpoint returns new token, second /v1/chat returns full stream. assert final output contains expected sentinel. keeps CI fast.

noah: i'll draft the msw test. sketch:```js
import { rest } from 'msw';
import { setupServer } from 'msw/node';
// handlers: first chat => 401, token refresh => 200 with new token, chat retry => 200 full stream
```

infra-bot: Created issue #2276: "examples/minimal-next-langchain: edge + token rotation occasionally causes 401 and truncated eval output" assigned to chloe — https://issues.redwood/2276

devx-bot: PR opened: examples/minimal-next-langchain#12 — bump @redwood/sdk -> 0.12.3; add env guard and logging; add msw test. CI pending.

pri: once PR lands, i'll re-run the harness and unflag the flaky job if green. also add a one-liner in quickstart telling users to prefer server runtime for early experiments or set env properly for edge.

chloe: tiny follow-up: consider ship a starter checklist that runs a local msw smoke during pre-commit for examples so we catch regressions before CI. low overhead.

noah: agreed. thanks team — this one was an onboarding friction that would've cost new users time. small fixes with good ROI. :sparkles:
